los that are all coming  together into a uniform searchable  output then we can actually start  learning new things of the data so for  example we may want to research an  indication acute lymphoblastic leukemia  so we go into our database we want to  say what does our organization done on  this before alright so we can there's 40  studies that's been done at different  locations across our organization  there's some other things I think I want  to look at this particular inhibitor and  I want to see a map view of it so if we  look at this kind of cartoon version of  a mind map we can really drill into one  particular study and again start  reaching out to different parts at the  data to start asking questions will show  me all the phenotypes show me what was  molecularly done and even drill down  piece by piece to get more and more data  to associate with this particular point  so we can take that particular study and  maybe we want to drill into it a  different way so we have the same study  we were just visualizing with the mind  map but now we want to look at a  dataview so just show me everything that  we've got in the database on this  particular study actually so I want to  drill down and some ice there's 35 mice  associated with it it's going to shows  us all of their names we can choose the  ones that are of interest and maybe we  want to know what their genotypes were  what what assays have been run what the  calls were what phenotyping has been  done what were the IOT what were the  sensor saying about their environmental  conditions at that time and did it have  a impact on their microbiome let's say  so now we have three mice that has data  on that you can see we're drilling into  further and further levels to get  exactly what we want to see and  overlaying totally different data silos  together to get information in one and  this is the type of thing that you can  then export out to your favorite nowis  engine like power bi or Spotfire work a  Google 